Trading hiatus — deliberate halt.

Since:   2026-06 (owner decision; confirmed 2026-07-15 during the fresh-eyes audit)

Why:     The prod Alpaca account was fully liquidated — positions closed, cash
         withdrawn, account inert. Economics are under review (audit finding
         0.2: lifetime gross trading P&L ~= -$35 against ~$150-250 of API
         spend), and the Tier 1 money-path fixes were prerequisites for
         re-enabling the cron.

Effect:  run-docker.sh exits 0 while this file exists, so the cron session and
         weekly learn jobs are no-ops. ALGO_TRADING_HALTED in .env is the
         in-container twin, covering `task session` and manual invocations.
         Neither is a failure: both log loudly and exit 0.

Before this file existed, the halt lived only in a hand-edited installed
crontab — uncommitted, undated, and identical in appearance to a system that
had silently stalled. That ambiguity is the thing this file exists to remove.

Resume:  docs/runbook-recovery.md, "Halt / Resume". Short version: review the
         audit's Tier 1 / 0.2 status, `rm HALT`, clear ALGO_TRADING_HALTED,
         uncomment the session lines in `crontab`, reinstall with
         `crontab /home/jay/dev/algo/crontab`, and commit — a resume should be
         as visible in git as the halt.

         Note: re-depositing capital will read as a giant equity jump to the
         dashboard and the reflection stage, which cannot distinguish deposits
         from performance (audit B.6/2.6).
